# /etc/rc.de-user.linux — the DE-spawned shell's namespace, on the Linux line.
#
# Staged into the image as /etc/rc.de-user. It is NOT etc/rc.de-user, Hamnix's
# own, and the differences are all things that do not exist here YET rather
# than things this line does differently:
#
#   * `bind '#I' /net` — the /net file server is not written (HANDOFF §3).
#     Hamnix's version binds it here, and its comment is right that a terminal
#     without /net cannot ssh out. That comes with the /net work.
#   * `bind '#r/home' /home` — #r is the boot file server; on this line the
#     initramfs IS the root, so /home is already there.
#   * `bind '#w' /dev/win` — the per-window file server. On this line a client
#     finds its window through /dev/wsys/self, which the compositor already
#     answers, so there is nothing to bind.
# `setuid 1001` USED TO BE on that list — "there is no provisioned `live` user
# in the image yet". There is now: scripts/hamlinux_image.sh stages
# /etc/passwd + /etc/group + /etc/shadow and creates /home/live owned by
# uid 1001 in the initramfs, so the account genuinely exists and the drop at
# the bottom of this file is real. See the long note down there for why the
# drop is at that exact point and not earlier.
#
# What IS kept is the shape: the terminal's shell gets its capabilities by
# having things bound into its namespace, and the templates are captured but
# not entered, so the user can reach the Debian subsystem on demand.
#
# `debian` IS THE NAME. The command is `enter debian { sh }`. That is what the
# namespace actually is -- a Debian bookworm root, built by mmdebstrap in
# scripts/hamlinux_distro.sh -- and naming it `linux` was a category error
# twice over: the kernel underneath BOTH namespaces is Linux, so `linux` does
# not distinguish anything, and a user typing it is not asking for a kernel,
# they are asking for apt and Firefox. `linux` stays as an alias because
# etc/install.hamsh.linux and several tests spell it that way, and because a
# name that used to work should keep working; it is not the one to teach.
#
# Syntax: hamsh, not bash. '#' starts a comment; device-letter names like '#c'
# MUST be single-quoted. `bind SRC DST` is source-first.

echo 'rc.de-user: planting the shell namespace'

bind '#c' /dev
bind '#p' /proc
bind '#s' /srv
bind '#/' /n

# EVERY distribution namespace, captured as a template and entered on demand.
# Nothing is granted by capturing one: `ns clean { }` is a description, and
# what an `enter` switches to is that distribution's root, never the native
# one. A session that never types either word pays nothing for having both.
#
# This used to be the same five lines written out once per distribution, in
# this file AND in etc/rc.boot.linux AND in etc/rc.boot.installed -- three
# copies that had to be kept in step by hand, and were not: the installed one
# had none of them. /etc/rc.distros is generated from /etc/distros by
# scripts/hamlinux_image.sh and sourced by all three, so `enter alpine { sh }`
# means the same thing on the console, in a desktop terminal, and after a
# reboot into an installed disk, by construction rather than by diligence.
source '/etc/rc.distros'
# The alias, kept working, deliberately after the real names. An alias is not
# a row in the table, so it is written out rather than generated.
linux = ns clean {
    bind '#distro/debian' /
    bind '#c' /dev
    bind '#p' /proc
    bind '#s' /srv
    bind '#/' /n
}

echo 'rc.de-user: namespace ready'

# --- DROP PRIVILEGE: the session is not the machine's owner ----------
# Everything above this line needed privilege and everything below it must
# not have it. That is the whole reason the drop is HERE and not two lines
# earlier or later:
#
#   * ABOVE: `bind` on this line performs a REAL Linux mount(2) (see the
#     device-server table in user/linux-syscalls.c) and mount(2) needs
#     CAP_SYS_ADMIN. Dropping first would make every bind above fail with
#     EPERM and hand the terminal a shell with no /dev, no /proc and no
#     `linux` template — the exact empty-namespace bug etc/rc.de-user was
#     written to fix, just with a different cause.
#   * BELOW: the interactive REPL, and any program hamUId asked us to run.
#     Those are the user's, and they must be the user's identity.
#
# WHO WE DROP TO. uid 1001 is `live`, the live image's default regular
# account, provisioned in /etc/passwd + /etc/group + /etc/shadow with home
# /home/live (staged into the initramfs, owned by 1001). It is the same
# shape as Ubuntu's `ubuntu` live user and the same uid Hamnix's rc.boot
# live branch drops to, so a desktop terminal reports `live` from `whoami`
# on either kernel. docs/security.md F10-3 is the rule being obeyed: an
# interactive session must NEVER run as the host owner.
#
# WHY THIS IS SAFE AND ONE-WAY. setuid is a privilege-DROP primitive. On
# this line PID 1 is uid 0 because the Linux kernel gives us no choice
# about that, so the chain is 0 -> 1001 and the kernel drops the real,
# effective and saved uids together: nothing below can climb back.
#
# WHAT STAYS PRIVILEGED, deliberately:
#   * PID 1 (the hamsh that ran /etc/rc.boot, and the console shell it
#     falls into). It mounts, loads modules, configures the interface and
#     runs the installer. rc.boot.linux says so where it decides not to
#     drop.
#   * wsysd, the compositor. It opens /dev/fb (DRM master), reads the evdev
#     input devices and owns the VT. Those are root-only on Linux and the
#     screen goes black without them. It is the trusted part of the DE, the
#     way an X server or a Wayland compositor on a seat is.
#   * hamdesktop / hampanelscene, started from rc.5 alongside the
#     compositor. They are system chrome, not the person's programs: the
#     panel spawns the terminals and the launchers, which is precisely the
#     privileged act of handing a session to a user. On Hamnix the chrome's
#     authority is a uid gate in devwsys.ad; making it unprivileged there
#     needs a real trusted-compositor capability rather than a setuid line
#     (see the closing note in etc/rc.de-user). On THIS line that gate is
#     now ported — see THE UID GATE below for what it does and does not
#     cover. Staying root is what makes them the host owner, so it is load-
#     bearing rather than incidental.
# What that leaves unprivileged is exactly the right thing: the shell the
# person types into, and every program they start from it. `hpm install`
# from a desktop terminal now fails to write /bin, which is the point.
#
# The user's programs can still DRAW after the drop, which is the point that
# had to be got right: /dev/wsys is a shared segment in the 1777 tmpfs on
# /srv, and user/linux-wsys.c chmods it 0666 when it is created, so a client
# of any uid can attach and map its window. Without that chmod the drop would
# have produced a session that is unprivileged and BLIND -- the client would
# silently fall through to a private /tmp segment and draw into a screen
# nobody composites.
#
# THE UID GATE IS NOW PORTED, and this is what it does to the paragraph
# above. Hamnix's devwsys.ad rejects the system-chrome ctl verbs (appmenu,
# setapp, run, lock, notif, ...) from any uid but the owner's; until
# recently user/linux-wsys.c had NO uid check at all and the file mode was
# the whole access control, so `live` could drive chrome verbs it should not
# be able to drive. That gap is closed: see THE UID GATE in
# user/linux-wsys.c, and tests/linux/wsys_uidgate.sh, which drives every one
# of those verbs from an unprivileged uid and requires a refusal, while
# requiring that the same uid can still map and draw its OWN window.
#
# The host owner is the uid that OWNS THE SEGMENT, which is why the chrome
# above still works: rc.5 starts wsysd before anything drops, so /srv/wsys
# belongs to root, and hamdesktop/hampanelscene are root too. It also means
# a single-uid run (the offscreen harness, a host test) refuses nothing,
# because there the one uid is the owner.
#
# FOR THE SYSTEM CHROME, THE KERNEL NOW ENFORCES IT. What used to be written
# here as the honest limit -- that this is a check inside a LIBRARY, so a
# program which bypasses the file protocol and mmaps /srv/wsys itself is bound
# by nothing -- was closed by splitting the state in two. /srv/wsys stays 0666
# and holds the WINDOW TABLE, because a uid-1001 client must be able to attach
# and map its own window or the paragraph above is undone. /srv/wsys.chrome is
# 0644 and owned by the host owner, and holds the screen geometry and the
# chrome sinks (lock, run, notif, appmenu, setapp, cycler, ws, kbd, tray, ...);
# non-owners map it PROT_READ, so they can still READ the model they render and
# the kernel refuses them the write -- protocol or no protocol. THE SPLIT in
# user/linux-wsys.c states the rule for which state goes where, and
# tests/linux/wsys_bypass.sh drives a program that skips the protocol entirely
# and measures both halves.
#
# WHAT IS STILL TRUE. The window table itself is still world-writable and has
# to be, so a bypasser can still retitle another client's window, scribble its
# scene or inject into its key ring -- wsys_bypass.sh now drives all three as
# measurements, read back through the protocol, so the day any of them closes
# the test says so.
#
# AND A FOURTH, WHICH NEEDS NO WRITE AT ALL AND SO NO MODE CAN CLOSE. The three
# above are integrity and each needs PROT_WRITE. The table also has to be
# world-READABLE -- the panel taskbar parses every window's title out of
# /dev/wsys/windows -- and that alone is enough for one uid-1001 process to read
# another's KEYSTROKES straight out of its `keys` ring, without disturbing the
# ring, so the victim receives them normally and cannot tell. Its committed
# scene (what is drawn inside it) and its whole row read the same way.
# wsys_bypass.sh drives it O_RDONLY as attack 4 of 4. This is why the fix
# cannot be "one table at 0644 with the writes behind an RPC": that would close
# 1-3 and leave a keylogger between the terminal and the browser on this line.
#
# And which of the two recorded fixes actually closes it, now measured rather
# than left open: NOT a mapping per owner-uid. This line is the reason -- it
# drops the WHOLE session to uid 1001, so the terminal, the browser and a
# malicious download are all uid 1001, and Unix file permissions cannot tell
# two processes of one uid apart. A per-uid mapping would only re-draw the
# root-vs-live boundary the 0644 chrome segment already draws; it does nothing
# about one uid-1001 app spoofing another, which is the attack a real desktop
# faces. wsys_bypass.sh's same-uid case proves it lands. The only fix that
# distinguishes two same-uid processes is the window table moving behind an RPC
# to wsysd, which authenticates each request's peer pid against the window's
# owner -- a subsystem, not an edit, and THE SPLIT in user/linux-wsys.c records
# why a partial version (a title-only RPC) is unsound and must not be shipped.
#
# WHAT THAT SUBSYSTEM WOULD COST, now measured instead of feared. The reason it
# was left was that an RPC per frame and per keystroke would put wsysd on every
# client's hot path. tests/linux/wsys_write_census.sh counts every mutation of
# both segments off a real offscreen desktop and the fear does not survive it:
# a whole session -- bring-up, ten idle seconds and twelve under a mouse --
# makes FIFTEEN lifecycle writes in TOTAL (3 newwindow, 6 geometry, 5 attr, 1
# focus) against 771 per-frame ones, 435:1 while the mouse moves, and an IDLE
# desktop makes ZERO. Identity and lifecycle behind an authority is affordable
# by two orders of magnitude. What is NOT affordable, and what attack 4 above
# forces, is that the per-frame half must move too -- into per-window memory a
# non-owner cannot map at all (a memfd handed out at window creation), because
# a shared table that anyone may read is a keylogger no matter who may write it.
# THE SPLIT records that boundary field by field, and what an attacker can
# still do after it.
echo 'rc.de-user: dropping the session to regular user live (uid 1001)'
setuid 1001

# $HOME follows the identity. For the interactive-REPL case hamsh main()
# would work this out for itself (_set_home_from_passwd resolves uid 1001 ->
# /home/live out of /etc/passwd), but the HAMNIX_DE_PROG branch below exits
# before main() ever reaches that hook, so an exec'd DE program would get
# the placeholder "/" and write its dotfiles into the filesystem root.
#
# Two statements, not one: `export NAME=VALUE` is not hamsh — the tokenizer
# splits on '=' and the stray '=' is a parse error that would abort the rest
# of this rc, so the DE program below would never be started at all. The
# value MUST be quoted too: an assignment RHS is parsed as an expression, so
# a bare /home/live is read as arithmetic ('/' = divide) and yields empty.
HOME='/home/live'
export HOME

# hamUId / hamtermscene plant HAMNIX_DE_PROG before exec'ing us. Set means
# "run that program in this window"; unset means "be an interactive shell",
# which is what a terminal wants.
if $HAMNIX_DE_PROG:
    if $HAMNIX_DE_PROG_ARG1:
        $HAMNIX_DE_PROG $HAMNIX_DE_PROG_ARG1
    else:
        $HAMNIX_DE_PROG
    exit $status
